from pathlib import Path
import json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];OUT=ROOT/'review-revision/sentence-review-implementation'
app=(ROOT/'manuscript/appendix.tex').read_text();actions=json.loads((OUT/'text-actions.json').read_text())
def change(old,new,ids):
    global app
    assert app.count(old)==1,(ids,old[:80],app.count(old))
    app=app.replace(old,new);actions.append({'file':'manuscript/appendix.tex','review_ids':ids,'old':old,'new':new})
def paragraph(start,new,ids):
    at=app.index('\n'+start)+1;old=app[at:].split('\n\n')[0];change(old,new,ids)

change('The first and second thirds contain respectively 574/574 RewardBench prompts or 185/185 WMT segments; the test remainder has 575 prompts or 187 segments.','RewardBench reference and calibration sets each contain 574 prompts, leaving 575 for testing. WMT23 reference and calibration sets each contain 185 source segments, leaving 187 for testing.',[109])
change('The source-model identity mapping is included with the analysis files; a results dataset revision does not identify a model-weight checkpoint revision.','Analysis files record the source-model identity mapping. A results-dataset revision alone does not identify the corresponding model-weight checkpoints.',[110])
change('None of these logical score counts is a physical GPU call count. In particular, reward scoring can batch completions, and document-level metrics can share computation across segments.','These entry counts are not GPU-call counts: reward models can batch completions, and document-level metrics can share computation across segments.',[113])
change('We calculate ratios within each split before averaging, rather than dividing averaged counts.','Reported ratios and paired differences are computed within each split before aggregation.',[114])
change('Differences use unrounded split values, and display rounding is applied after aggregation.','Display rounding is applied only after aggregation.',[114])
change('The composition experiment has one empty-active cell: full WMT23, random pool 04, median, $q=.05$, all 40 splits.','In composition sensitivity, thirteen-stream WMT23 with random pool 04, median pooling and $q=.05$ has an empty active set in all 40 splits.',[115])
change(r'\section{Full-Information Controls}',r'\section{Comparison Methods}',[116])
change('Zero-query retention replaces $F_i$ by its upper bound $Tp_i$ and solves selected cardinality. It spends no candidate-test inspections.','Zero-query retention maximizes returned baseline-selected count using upper costs $Tp_i$, with no candidate-test queries.',[117])
change('It optimizes all-decision coverage.','It maximizes committed-decision count, including exclusions.',[117])
change('Batch selected retention applies that sort only to the base selected outputs.','Batch selected retention maximizes returned baseline-selected count using true costs $F_i$, applying the sort only to selected outputs.',[117])
paragraph('Zero is always feasible',r'Zero is always feasible and includes zero-disagreement items. Under conditional group exchangeability, parameter-dependent CRC bounds expected loss \citep{angelopoulos2024crc,yeh2025conformal}; individual batches may exceed $\alpha$. The query certificate instead enforces the current batch\textquotesingle s budget within the declared replacement family under every consistent score completion.',[118])
change(r'\section{Matched-Throughput Preference Diagnostic}',r'\section{Preference Diagnostic at Matched Returned Count}',[119,120])
change('For each returned mask with count $K$, the quality analysis compares its precision with the $K$ highest base-consensus scores.','For a returned set of size $K$, we compare its preference precision with that of the $K$ outputs having the highest base-consensus scores.',[119])
paragraph('The diagnostic was specified after',r'The diagnostic was specified after the inspection protocol and is not used to tune acquisition or stopping. For each split, the comparator matches returned count but is not constrained by the churn budget. Averaging and rounding follow Appendix~\ref{app:protocol}. All RewardBench comparisons have 40 defined splits; supplied WMT23 panels contain no preference or translation-quality labels.'+'\n'+r'\input{tables/query-quality}',[120])
change('The trace of both frontiers shows how inspection order affects certification within the same batch.','The following trace illustrates how inspection order affects certification within one batch.',[121])
change('This trace illustrates the observed ordering difference while holding the risk budget, base geometry and certificate criterion fixed.','The batch, risk budget and certificate criterion stay fixed; the trace illustrates the ordering difference without identifying its cause.',[121])
change('Each order can therefore be shared, while each budget retains its own first positive-gap and exact stopping state.','Each order is reused across budgets, with separate first positive-gap and exact stopping states for each budget.',[122])
change('Twenty unique sorted seven-member subsets exclude the original pool.','We sample 20 distinct seven-member pools, excluding the original pool as a complete combination while allowing its members in other combinations.',[123])
change('Overlapping splits and pools are never converted to iid confidence intervals or independent dataset counts.','Pool-level quantiles summarize sensitivity across sampled pools, not population confidence intervals.',[124])
change('The acquisition record pins the archive and all used files.','The acquisition record identifies the archive and files used in the analysis.',[128])
paragraph('There are 171, 171 and 197',r'The en-de, en-es and ja-zh panels contain 171, 171 and 197 source documents, respectively. Seed $924000+r$ shuffles document names. The first third forms reference, the second forms unused calibration, and the remainder forms test. Unequal document sizes make test count $N$ and nominal $k$ vary across splits. ECDF maps and cutoff use all outputs in reference documents. Tie and random-query seeds add 700001 and 99173; pool size, ridge penalty, features and tolerance retain the development settings. We evaluate all three admitted directions. The pre-results freeze covered the risk objective and panel-inclusion criteria; the later numerical repair is disclosed above. Evaluator-training independence is not claimed.',[129,130])
change('Their split means describe concentration within that family. They are not per-replacement guarantees, and averaging split maxima does not give the maximum across all splits.','Their splitwise means describe variation across replacements, without a per-replacement guarantee. The mean of split-specific maxima is not the maximum over all splits.',[135])
change('We adapt its marginal criterion, since a selected-count denominator would change the current target.','We use its marginal criterion because churn has a fixed batch denominator. A selected-output denominator would control a different quantity.',[136])
paragraph('Thus SCoRE-MDR with target',r'For equal-size groups, the identity converts churn into $B=N/(2k)$ times average group loss. SCoRE-MDR at target $\alpha/B$ therefore bounds expected churn by $\alpha$, assuming conditional whole-group exchangeability after independent reference fitting. This algebraic reduction does not establish that a fixed benchmark satisfies the assumption. The method accepts a group\textquotesingle s selected outputs together, unlike output-level retention.',[137])
change('The same candidate-specific ridge/features predict calibration and test flip costs.','The same candidate-specific ridge model and feature definitions predict calibration and test flip costs.',[138])
change('These checks support the implementation and do not replace the published theorem or a group-generating assumption.','These checks assess implementation consistency; they neither replace the published theorem nor establish group exchangeability.',[139])
paragraph('The resulting expectation comparison',r'Among 4,800 fixed-panel records, 70 exceed their batch budget. These exceedances alone do not contradict an expectation guarantee. High coverage without candidate test queries likewise does not establish the deterministic completion certificate. Grouped SCoRE requires fully scored base and candidate calibration data; query certification uses reference and base-test information plus the candidate test scores it inspects. These costs are recorded separately.',[140])
paragraph('WMT24 splits keep whole',r'WMT24 documents contain unequal numbers of outputs. An unweighted document-level MDR objective would average document risks rather than reproduce the output-average loss, so we do not apply this equal-size-group reduction there. A weighted adaptation requires a separate derivation. Scalar panels also lack SCOPE\textquotesingle s bidirectional judgment probabilities and Bidirectional Preference Entropy (BPE) \citep{badshah2026scope}. Preference or incomplete MQM labels do not supply the full relative ranks required by transductive full-ranking inference \citep{fermanian2025transductive}. We therefore do not present scalar-margin proxies as reproductions of these methods.'+'\n'+'\n'.join(r'\input{tables/review-score-'+str(i)+'-'+agg+'}' for i in range(3) for agg in ['median','mean']),[141])
paragraph('Each configuration records',r'Each configuration records $Mn_{\rm ref}$ base-reference entries, $Tn_{\rm ref}$ candidate-reference entries and $MN$ base-test entries. CRC and grouped SCoRE also record fully scored base and candidate calibration entries. The earlier WMT23 correlation check reads all $24\times557\times13=173{,}784$ retained scores, including outputs later assigned to test splits. It is excluded from post-freeze counts, so replay does not establish acquisition savings from the start of panel construction. Reference scoring could be reused across batches only while fitted maps, cutoff protocol and declared family remain applicable. The 40 independently refitted splits are not such a deployment sequence.'+'\n\n'+r'Removing a candidate changes the certified family. A claim about the original family still needs conservative treatment of its unobserved scores. Endpoint bounds avoid queries while retaining the candidate in the family.',[142])
change('Within each reference split, a fixed nested prefix of the original shuffled groups supplies all, half or quarter of the reference groups, rounding down with at least one group.','Within each reference split, nested prefixes of the original shuffled group order retain all, half or one quarter of the reference groups. Fractional group counts are rounded down, with at least one group retained.',[143])
change('These analyses on exposed panels are not prospective preregistration.','These analyses were conducted on previously inspected panels and were not prospectively preregistered.',[147])
change('This static priority uses reference and base information, never hidden candidate-test scores.',r'This static column priority uses reference and visible base information only, not hidden candidate test scores. It aggregates the entry method\textquotesingle s per-cell predictions at the column level.',[148])
paragraph('Observed score correlations may',r'Observed score correlations may guide query order without changing the endpoint certificate. Using relations among missing scores to tighten bounds requires guarantees that those relations hold for the true scores; learned low-rank approximations or concentrated posteriors alone provide no such guarantee. Both joint endpoint vectors are attainable in the unrestricted model, so interior point estimates cannot tighten the frontiers. A margin ruling out every single-member flip is already captured by $p_i=0$; base-pool variance alone does not restrict missing candidate scores. Nonuniform replacement weights preserve additive churn but change evidence optimization. Simultaneous replacements change median boundary geometry. Neither extension inherits the current unit-count witness formulas.',[151])
change('Holding the numerical value of $\\alpha$ fixed instead changes the feasible budget.','Keeping the same numerical $\\alpha$ instead changes the allowed flip count.',[152])
change('An offline witness receives the true matrix and selects evidence with hindsight. We measure discovery overhead as','With the true matrix known, offline construction selects evidence with hindsight. We define discovery overhead as',[155])
change(r'let $Y\in\{0,1\}^{m\times T}$ contain the selected boundary opposition bits and define $V_B(w)=\max\{|C|:C\subseteq\mathcal B,\ \sum_{i\in C}w_i\le b\}$ and let $L=V_B(z)$ be the true optimal boundary count.',r'let $Y\in\{0,1\}^{m\times T}$ contain their opposition bits. Under bit budget $b$, define boundary capacity $V_B(w)=\max\{|C|:C\subseteq\mathcal B,\ \sum_{i\in C}w_i\le b\}$ and true optimum $L=V_B(z)$.',[157])
change('For each column subset, intersection popcounts give a histogram of observed costs.','For each column subset, counting opposition bits in its intersection with each row mask gives a histogram of observed costs.',[160])
change(r'With $P$ distinct row supports, worst-case time is $O(mT+2^T(P+T))$ and space is $O(m+2^T+P+T)$ beyond the input.',r'With $D_{\rm sup}$ distinct row supports, worst-case time is $O(mT+2^T(D_{\rm sup}+T))$ and space is $O(m+2^T+D_{\rm sup}+T)$ beyond the input.',[160])
change('The entry-count lower bound and feasible column stops prune cardinalities.','The entry lower bound and feasible online column counts restrict the subset sizes requiring enumeration.',[161])
change('Certificate feasibility is monotone under further revelation, so rejecting that entire level excludes every smaller subset.','Revealing more columns cannot invalidate a certificate, so failure of every subset at that size rules out every smaller subset.',[161])
change('Thus entry complexity depends on row sums, while column complexity also depends on where their evidence co-occurs.','Row sums determine entry certificate size; column certificate size also depends on whether the needed evidence occurs in the same columns.',[162])
change('Candidate opposition supports are sets covering the boundary rows, and the minimum witness is precisely their minimum set cover.','Associate each candidate column with the rows where it has an opposition. The minimum column certificate is a minimum set cover of the boundary rows.',[163])
change('All six original full-reference right-ECDF median panels, four depths and 40 splits run at $\\alpha=.10$, giving 960 exact instances.','Exact column witnesses use all six original panels with median pooling, full-reference right ECDF and $\\alpha=.10$. Four depths and 40 splits give 960 instances.',[164])
change('These minima show that much of the column cost remains even when the witness knows all outcomes. Online overhead describes discovery on top of this intrinsic cost.','For these RewardBench and thirteen-stream WMT23 configurations, minimum certificates still require a large fraction of scores even when evidence is chosen with full knowledge of the matrix. Online counts additionally include the cost of discovering suitable columns.',[165])
